<$fill $name="body">

!! Custom widget registration

Each widget JS file exports via `exports["json-obj"]` (etc.), which TiddlyWiki registers as `<$json-obj>`. The `module-type: widget` tells TW to load these as widget constructors at boot.

!! parentWidget traversal

The `getJsonContext(widget)` helper walks up the widget tree via `widget.parentWidget` until it finds a widget that owns a `jsonBuilderContext`. This is how nested widgets find the shared builder context without passing data through attributes.

!! refreshSelf pattern

All JSON DSL widgets call `this.refreshSelf()` in their `refresh()` method, causing a full re-execute of the subtree. This keeps the JSON tree stateless — every render produces a fresh tree from current widget attributes and filter results.

!! wiki.setText

`<$json-save>` uses `$tw.wiki.setText(title, field, null, value)` to write the serialized JSON into a tiddler field. This triggers TW's change detection and reactive refresh cycle for any widgets that depend on the target tiddler.

!! $tw.wiki.filterTiddlers

`<$json-array>` and `<$json-prop>` accept `filter` attributes. The filter is evaluated via `$tw.wiki.filterTiddlers(filter, widget)`, where the widget reference provides the current variable scope. This means filter expressions can reference variables set by parent widgets (e.g., `$let`, `$list`).

!! Widget.computeAttribute

All widget attributes are resolved via TW's standard `computeAttribute` pipeline — supporting literal values, transclusions (`{{tiddler!!field}}`), macro substitutions (`<<variable>>`), and filter attributes (`{{{ [filter] }}}`).

